// _tests_/bit/benchgate/baselinesflip.bit -- the per-case allocated-object
// counts each bench case reports when it is compiled with #3901's string
// flip ON (`BIT_STRING_EXPLODE_TEST=1`, read by `lowerProject` in
// compiler/lowerglobal.bit).
//
// #4508. Sibling in the same directory module as `baselines.bit`,
// `baselinesdefs.bit`, `baselineslinux.bit`, `benchgate.bit` and
// `benchrun.bit`: no `import`, one shared scope. It is a NEW file rather than
// a field on `Baseline` because `baselines.bit` sits at 787 of the 800-line
// E0200 limit and 13 cases x one field line would break it.
//
// WHY THIS TABLE EXISTS. Nothing else in the suite compiles a benchmark with
// the flip on, so #3901's question -- "is the flip shippable yet" -- had no
// mechanical answer: the flip could regress or improve any case by millions
// of objects and every gate would stay green. The numbers below are that
// answer, checked by `runFlipCase` in `benchgate.bit` for EXACT EQUALITY --
// not the [0.5x, 1.05x] band the flip-off baselines get; `checkFlipObjects`'s
// comment has the two reasons and the arithmetic. Three of these are debt,
// not targets; `flipDebt()` in `baselinesdefs.bit` says which, and asserts
// the split in both directions.
//
// MEASURED on main `e552d150` (#4423's merge, the newest child of #3901),
// macOS/arm64, one build + one run per case with BIT_STRING_EXPLODE_TEST=1
// exported to the compiler and BIT_GC_STATS=1 on the run, `swept+live`:
//
//   case     flip off    flip on
//   alloc    10006006    10006006   identical binaries (cmp rc=0)
//   json      5400061    22626099   debt: 3.77x
//   sort       600022    10135130   debt: 16.9x
//   strmap     400041    12850043   debt: 32.1x
//   map             14          14   identical binaries (cmp rc=0)
//   matrix          16          16   identical binaries (cmp rc=0)
//   strings   7125035          38   the flip's whole point: 187,500x fewer
//
// RE-MEASURED UNCHANGED on `8de0cab2`, the commit this table was committed
// against -- 7 merges and 28 commits later, including #4421's store-table
// barrier and two generics fixes. Both readings are on #4508.
//
// EVERY LANDING CHILD OF #3901 MOVES THESE, and #4423 moved two of them
// between this ticket's design and its landing: json read 32151094 and strmap
// 12850044 on `a820b711`, one merge earlier. Re-measure before quoting them,
// and name the commit.
//
// #4423's json move is also why this arm checks equality. It is -29.6%:
// against the old 32151094 the new reading sits UNDER the 1.05x ceiling
// (33758648) and OVER the 0.5x floor (16075547), so a table describing a
// compiler two merges old would have passed the flip-off arm's band
// unnoticed. That band absorbs doubling-scale movement, which is right for a
// noisy shipped baseline and wrong for a deterministic debt figure.
//
// DETERMINISM, verified rather than assumed. On `e552d150` all seven flip-on
// binaries were re-run and reported identical counts (14 of 14 readings). On
// `8de0cab2` the seven numbers above and the seven the gate itself reports
// come from INDEPENDENT builds in separate scratch trees, and agree exactly.
//
// The three cases the flip does not move (alloc, map, matrix) build
// BYTE-IDENTICAL binaries with and without it (`cmp` rc=0, output paths of
// equal length so Mach-O's embedded name cannot skew it), while json, sort,
// strmap and strings all differ (`cmp` rc=1). That is also the proof the flag
// reaches the COMPILER and not merely the measured run: a flag that reached
// only the run would leave all seven pairs identical.
//
// #4424 (the RETURN half of the ABI) then moved exactly ONE of the seven, by
// exactly ONE object: strmap 12850043 -> 12850042. Re-measured on
// `e324eba7`, both compilers built from the same tree, one build and one run
// per reading:
//
//   case     flip off   flip on, #4423 only   flip on, with #4424
//   alloc    10006006             10006006             10006006
//   json      5400061             22626099             22626099
//   sort       600022             10135130             10135130
//   strmap     400041             12850043             12850042   <- the move
//   map             14                   14                   14
//   matrix          16                   16                   16
//   strings   7125035                   38                   38
//
// THAT NEGATIVE RESULT IS THE FINDING, not a measurement failure, and it is
// #4443's lesson again from the other side. Exploding a RETURN does not
// remove an allocation, it MOVES it: the callee stops boxing and whichever
// caller wants a whole `string` boxes instead. The object disappears only
// when the consumer is itself an exploded position -- another local, an
// argument to an eligible callee, or another return. json's `internKey`
// result becomes a MAP KEY, a container position boxed by design until
// #4426, so its 600,000 calls lose three static `bit_rt_gc_alloc` call sites
// each (7 -> 4 of that function's 14 -> 11 BR26 relocations) and zero
// dynamic objects. strmap's single `buildBlob()` call is the one whose result
// binds to an exploded local, and it is worth exactly one object.
//
// So the return half pays in INSTRUCTIONS, not in this table: json's object
// drops 29412 -> 28395 `__TEXT,__text` instructions and its whole-object
// `bit_rt_gc_alloc` BR26 sites 556 -> 477 of 1738 -> 1660 total.
//
// #4426 (the CONTAINER boundary) then moved four of the seven, and it is the
// row that closes most of the debt. Re-measured on `76f0d674` with both
// compilers built from the same tree, one build and one run per reading,
// `BIT_STRING_EXPLODE_TEST=1` exported to the COMPILER and `BIT_GC_STATS=1`
// on the run:
//
//   case     flip off   flip on, before #4426   flip on, with #4426
//   alloc    10006006             10006006             10006006
//   json      5400061             22626099              6750063
//   sort       600022             10135130               600022   <- == flip off
//   strmap     400041             12850042               400041   <- == flip off
//   map             14                   14                   14
//   matrix          16                   16                   16
//   strings   7125035                   38                   37
//
// #4431 inserted `unbox(val)` immediately followed by `box(words)` at every
// container store and load. The value at all of those positions is ALREADY
// the boxed handle (`lowerExpr` returns one SSA id, and every multi-word
// position rebuilds the box before it can yield one), so the round trip
// reconstructed the object it had just taken apart — and the `field_get`s
// read a heap object nothing in the block stored, which store-to-load
// forwarding cannot answer, so the `gc_alloc` survived. One object per gated
// crossing. `strmap`'s 12,450,000 string-keyed map operations were 1:1 with
// its 12,450,001-object excess.
//
// `sort` and `strmap` come back to their flip-off counts EXACTLY, which is
// the strongest form the check can take: the flip now costs those two rows
// nothing at all. `flipDebt()` drops to `["json"]` — json's residual
// 1,350,002 is NOT the container boundary. It is #4423/#4443 territory: an
// argument that reaches a callee which then needs the whole string again,
// and a runtime call with a one-boxed-word ABI.

// The flip-on `swept+live` count for `name` on `target`, or -1 for "no flip
// reading on file" -- which is every case on every platform but macOS/arm64
// (`hostTarget()` ordinal 2, see `platformName`), and every case not listed
// here. `runFlipCase` skips the whole second build when this returns -1, so
// the Linux tables in `baselineslinux.bit` need no edit and pay no cost.
//
// The seven cases here are the ones #4446 measured. `collatz`, `mandelbrot`,
// `fib`, `allocflat`, `churn` and `trustcache` are unmeasured, not exempt:
// adding a row is the only thing needed to bring one under the gate.
fn flipObjectsFor(name: string, target: int): int {
  if (target != 2) {
    return 0 - 1
  }
  if (name == "alloc") {
    return 10006006
  }
  if (name == "json") {
    return 6750063
  }
  if (name == "sort") {
    return 600022
  }
  if (name == "strmap") {
    return 400041
  }
  if (name == "map") {
    return 14
  }
  if (name == "matrix") {
    return 16
  }
  if (name == "strings") {
    return 37
  }
  return 0 - 1
}
